4601: rejected by a finite obstruction subrecord rather than accepted by this
4602: package.\end{proof}
4603: 
4604: \subsection{Current-package instantiation}
4605: 
4606: \noindent\emph{Six-row and seven-row states.}
4607: The current-package certificate state in this subsection is the six-row state
4608: $P^{+}_{6}$ with acceptance rows \path{a1}--\path{a6}.  The later
4609: theorem-obligation integration in
4610: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} is the seven-row extension
4611: $P^{+}_{7}$, obtained from $P^{+}_{6}$ by adding exactly the manual submitted
4612: theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}.  Thus the two theorems describe nested support states,
4613: not competing current-package mirrors.
4614: 
4615: \begin{theorem}[Current-package bounded schema-relative certificate exactness]
4616: \label{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}
4617: For the six-row finite current-package state $P^{+}_{6}$ of
4618: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, assume the
4619: support-surface availability condition of
4620: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the submitted supplemental
4621: material contains the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory with
4622: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, with refreshed digest rows and
4623: rerun finite-verifier logs for every named bundle coordinate regenerated after
4624: the digest surface was written.  Equivalently, in the sense of
4625: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity}, the theorem-environment extractor,
4626: certificate verifier, source-interface verifier, primary-claim verifier, and
4627: final digest surface must be a common finite support-surface fixed point after
4628: any regenerated inventory, log, or digest coordinate.  With that support
4629: surface present, the six ledger entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ as printed in
4630: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
4631: and as checked by \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} are
4632: exactly the accepted schema-relative finite-record instantiation of the finite-record interface theorem,
4633: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  More precisely, the package map
4634: \[
4635: a_i\longmapsto \mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)
4636: \]
4637: is a bijection from the accepted current-package claim rows printed in this
4638: source to the locally checkable certificates accepted by the submitted schema
4639: verifier at the declared path-verified evidence level, relative only to the
4640: finite fields read by that schema and to the manually declared six-row ledger
4641: classified in Theorem~\ref{thm:manual-ledger-dependence-boundary}.  Thus the
4642: exactness is a schema-relative and row-frozen audit relation between six printed
4643: rows and six finite certificates.  It proves agreement with the declared verifier
4644: schema, not semantic truth of package claims, and not correctness of the
4645: surrounding workflow, gate implementations, source tree, artifact evaluators,
4646: daemons, upload mechanisms, archives, or venues.  The assigned-gate rows are
4647: sound only in the finite-record sense of
4648: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}: they certify assigned
4649: gate coverage and declared evidence pointers, while any stronger assertion of
4650: independent gate implementation soundness would require source paths, commands,
4651: source states, environments, exit codes, and log paths for those gates.  Without
4652: that support surface, the theorem retains only the printed source-level inventory
4653: of six intended certificate rows
4654: and not the machine-readable current-package acceptance claim.  The durable TeX source
4655: fixes the six row statements and their negative boundaries; the supplemental
4656: record supplies the machine-readable mirror and verifier log only if it travels
4657: with the submission or with an archival replacement satisfying
4658: the final-support closure condition just stated.  For the CICM presentation-only
4659: software-paper reading of this package, this verifier domain is adequate in the
4660: following precise sense: the primary contribution uses these six rows only as
4661: finite audit records for source traceability, route packaging, intake isolation,
4662: overlap blocking, fake-extension rejection, and artifact-limitation disclosure;
4663: semantic theorem adequacy, truth of every package sentence, implementation
4664: correctness of the gate programs, fresh Lean/BEDC rebuilds, dynamic artifact
4665: validation, daemon behavior, public archive availability, and venue acceptance
4666: are not part of that declared contribution.  Every reviewer question inside the
4667: declared contribution is therefore a question about one of the six printed rows,
4668: its evidence level, its assigned pass rows, its boundary field, or the submitted
4669: support-surface condition; every reviewer question outside that list is a
4670: properly stronger reading and must pay the corresponding extension budget.  Every
4671: current-package reading requiring a seventh accepted row, a different claim kind,
4672: a stronger evidence level, an additional gate pass record, a fresh command-run
4673: tuple, an extractor-completeness assertion for the theorem inventory, or an
4674: external venue decision is outside this bijection and is rejected by
4675: the finite obstruction skeleton and, more sharply, by the positive extension-budget
4676: criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The exactness
4677: is therefore row-frozen, finite-record, and verifier-log bounded: editing any of
4678: the six rows or any machine-readable mirror changes the finite package unless the
4679: dependent extractor, verifier logs, and digest surface are rerun or regenerated
4680: into the common fixed point required by
4681: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity}, and the displayed tables and logs
4682: are updated together.
4683: \end{theorem}
4684: 
4685: \begin{proof}
4686: \emph{Construction from the displayed rows.}
4687: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger} constructs,
4688: for each row $a_i$ of Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
4689: and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}, a certificate
4690: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\).  The row set is finite and
4691: has exactly six elements.  The construction is injective because the row
4692: identifier, accepted claim text, and evidence surface in
4693: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} are part of the displayed record data;
4694: two different rows cannot have the same accepted current-package claim row.  It
4695: is surjective onto the accepted current-package certificates because the same
4696: proposition states that no load-bearing claim outside these six entries is
4697: accepted by the current-package instantiation.
4698: 
4699: \emph{Support-surface instantiation.}
4700: Conditioned on the supplemental records being present, matching the displayed
4701: paths, and matching the final digest manifest
4702: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
4703: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} supplies the finite
4704: schema instance over \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
4705: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
4706: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
4707: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script, and the
4708: recorded verifier log.  The refreshed recorded log states command
4709: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, source commit
4710: \path{current local automath checkout plus final package digest manifest review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, final package digest manifest
4711: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, environment \path{Python 3.10.11 on Windows 10}, exit code \path{0}, and log path
4712: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  The companion logs
4713: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log} and
4714: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log} likewise
4715: record exit code \path{0} against the same final digest manifest.  That instance checks kind-domain membership,
4716: path-verified evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
4717: pass-record fields, discovery-field discipline, and the path-verified command
4718: boundary for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  Hence every certificate in the image of
4719: the displayed package map is accepted by the submitted schema verifier at exactly
4720: the declared path-verified level when that supplemental record surface is part of
4721: the review package.  The support surface is finite:
4722: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} later proves that the bundle-backed
4723: component for this package is admissible exactly when the exact
4724: \path{review_bundle/} directory is submitted with its final digest manifest.
4725: Without that finite surface,
4726: the TeX proof still supplies the finite schema classification of the displayed
4727: rows, but not the additional claim that the submitted executable mirror and log
4728: were available to the reviewer.  By
4729: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, the same verification
4730: does not audit the implementation internals of the named gates: it verifies the
4731: presence and local evidence surfaces of the assigned finite pass rows, and a
4732: fresh implementation-soundness reading would be a command-result escalation not
4733: present in these six certificates.
4734: 
4735: \emph{Adequacy for the presentation route.}
4736: The route contribution described in the introduction and in
4737: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}--\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
4738: is finite-record auditability, not theorem proving or artifact semantics.  The
4739: six accepted rows enumerate exactly the finite records needed for that
4740: contribution: $a_1$ records the bounded imported-source interface, $a_2$ records
4741: the package and support-surface boundary, and $a_3$--$a_6$ record the four case
4742: lessons.  Hence any in-scope review of the current-package certificate asks
4743: whether these rows, their evidence levels, their pass rows, their discovery and
4744: boundary fields, and their submitted mirrors satisfy the schema.  That is exactly
4745: the predicate checked in the preceding paragraph.  A question about semantic
4746: truth of all package claims, proof adequacy, implementation soundness,
4747: fresh Lean/BEDC execution, dynamic artifact validation, daemon behavior, archive
4748: availability, or venue acceptance is not a missing case of the six-row verifier:
4749: it changes the claim kind, evidence level, support surface, or boundary field.
4750: By Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and the upgrade normal form of
4751: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a changed question is
4752: a properly stronger reading and is accepted only after its own finite extension
4753: coordinate and assigned pass row are supplied.  This proves the stated
4754: presentation-route adequacy without turning the row-frozen verifier into a
4755: semantic truth oracle.
4756: 
4757: \emph{Maximality and non-escalation.}
4758: Conversely, a stronger reading changes at least one finite component of the
4759: record: it adds a row, changes $K_i$, changes $\ell_i$, adds a required gate in
4760: $R_i$, replaces the path-verified boundary $B_i$ by a command-run tuple, or adds
4761: an external venue-decision surface.  Such a record is not in the six-row image
4762: just identified.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, a
4763: genuine strengthening must pay a positive finite extension budget in added
4764: coordinates, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton},
4765: the first unpaid coordinate gives a bad subrecord: a missing evidence surface,
4766: missing gate record, missing promotion or venue field, missing discovery-source
4767: row, or unsupported command-run escalation.  Thus the six certificates are not a
4768: sample of an unrecorded full-pipeline execution; they are exactly the row-frozen,
4769: finite, path-verified package instantiation of
4770: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.\end{proof}
4771: 
4772: 
4773: \begin{theorem}[Ledger dependence and semantic extractor-completeness boundary]
4774: \label{thm:manual-ledger-dependence-boundary}
4775: For the current package, the exactness assertions in
4776: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger},
4777: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, and
4778: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} are exactly finite-record
4779: claims over the displayed ledger rows, the submitted theorem-obligation
4780: inventory, and the syntactic theorem-environment extractor certificate of
4781: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}.  They
4782: therefore imply deterministic label coverage for the five environment classes in
4783: $\mathcal E$, but they imply neither proof semantics nor completeness for
4784: material outside that syntactic domain.  A stronger current-package claim of
4785: semantic extractor completeness is accepted by the same certificate calculus if
4786: and only if the support surface is enlarged by a finite semantic-completeness
4787: coordinate consisting of: the semantic extractor or proof-checking command,
4788: source commit, environment, exit code, log path, exact source file read, produced
4789: inventory file, digest binding between the source and produced inventory, and a
4790: gate pass row assigning that semantic record to the theorem-ledger or
4791: traceability claim.  In the present package that semantic coordinate is absent;
4792: therefore the accepted content remains the row-frozen certificate surface plus
4793: syntactic label coverage, and every semantic extractor-completeness reading has a
4794: finite bad subrecord.
4795: \end{theorem}
4796: 
4797: \begin{proof}
4798: Let \(M_6\) be the finite set of six displayed current-package ledger rows
4799: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\), and let \(M_7=M_6\cup\{a_7\}\) be the seven-row surface used by
4800: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  By
4801: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, the
4802: accepted current-package rows are constructed by reading the displayed row
4803: identifier, accepted claim, claim-kind set, evidence level, evidence surfaces,
4804: route boundary, pass records, discovery field, and command-boundary field.  By
4805: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, these six displayed rows
4806: are then mapped bijectively to the locally checkable certificates accepted by the
4807: schema verifier, conditioned on the submitted support surface.  These statements
4808: have no input coordinate for semantic proof checking; their inputs are exactly
4809: the finite row surfaces and submitted mirrors just listed.
4810: 
4811: The theorem-obligation extension adds the coordinate \(a_7\), which names the
4812: submitted theorem-ledger row, the manifest-exposed inventory file, and the
4813: syntactic extractor log.  Its proof checks manifest exposure, gate coverage,
4814: evidence pointers, the extractor's finite label comparison, and the boundary
4815: supplied by Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}.  Those
4816: checks turn a detached local inventory into a submitted finite obligation surface
4817: and eliminate the syntactic bad example of a labelled environment in
4818: $\mathcal E$ absent from the inventory.  They do not parse mathematical
4819: statements, replace the printed TeX proofs, or certify proof semantics.
4820: 
4821: Now consider a proposed stronger reading \(q\) asserting that the ledger or
4822: inventory is semantically extractor-complete for \path{main.tex}.  This reading
4823: is not record-equivalent to the accepted syntactic reading: it requires, at
4824: minimum, a new command-boundary tuple naming the semantic checker or extractor
4825: invocation, source state, environment, exit code, and log path; a
4826: source-to-inventory digest binding that identifies the TeX bytes read and the
4827: inventory bytes produced; and an assigned gate row saying that this semantic
4828: record is accepted for the theorem-ledger or traceability claim.  These are
4829: finite coordinates of the same kind as the extension coordinates classified by
4830: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  If they are present and
4831: pass their assigned gates, the enlarged tuple satisfies the six schema clauses
4832: A1--A6 and is accepted by Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.
4833: Conversely, if the stronger reading is accepted,
4834: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} forces it to differ
4835: from the syntactic reading in a coordinate read by the verifier; the only
4836: coordinates capable of expressing semantic extractor completeness are precisely
4837: the semantic command, digest, produced-inventory, source-file, and gate-pass
4838: records just listed.
4839: 
4840: For the current package, no such semantic-completeness coordinate is one of the
4841: six rows of \(M_6\), and the seventh row \(a_7\) supplies only the bounded
4842: theorem-ledger coordinate and syntactic extractor run.  Therefore the
4843: stronger semantic reading fails at the first missing schema coordinate.  By
4844: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, that failed coordinate
4845: has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord: the missing semantic command-run
4846: tuple, the missing source-to-inventory digest binding, the missing
4847: produced-inventory coordinate, or the missing assigned pass row.  Removing the
4848: stronger reading leaves the accepted finite-record and syntactic label-coverage
4849: claims unchanged.  Thus the boundary is exactly the one stated in the theorem.
4850: \end{proof}
4851: 
4852: \begin{corollary}[Row-frozen current-package invariance]
4853: \label{cor:row-frozen-current-package-invariance}
4854: Under the support-surface hypotheses of
4855: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current-package
4856: acceptance decision factors through the six displayed rows
4857: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) and their certificate fields only.  Any two ambient repository,
4858: daemon, artifact, or venue states that induce the same six tuples
4859: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the same submitted support
4860: surface, and the same verifier log path induce the same accepted current-package
4861: certificate set.  Conversely, a reading that depends on a seventh row, a modified
4862: row, a fresh command execution, semantic validity of BEDC/Lean/Rule110 artifacts,
4863: implementation soundness of the verifier or gates, upload status, or venue
4864: decision is not invariant under this six-row projection and is accepted only
4865: after it is added as a finite extension coordinate and rechecked.  Thus the
4866: current-package theorem is exactly a row-frozen finite-record theorem, not a
4867: current-execution reproducibility theorem and not a semantic-validity theorem.
4868: \end{corollary}
4869: 
4870: \begin{proof}
4871: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} identifies the accepted
4872: current-package certificates with the bijective image of the six rows
4873: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) under the map
4874: \(a_i\mapsto \mathcal C(a_i)\), conditioned on the submitted support surface and
4875: the recorded verifier log.  The verifier then reads only the finite fields of
4876: these tuples, the fixed dispatch table, and the cited submitted surfaces.  By
4877: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two ambient states
4878: with identical values on those finite coordinates have the same verifier
4879: decision and the same finite certificate set.  This proves the stated invariance.
4880: 
4881: For the converse boundary, each listed stronger reading changes a coordinate not
4882: contained in the six-row projection: a seventh accepted row changes the row set;
4883: a modified row changes one of \(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i\); a fresh command
4884: execution changes the boundary field to command-run evidence; semantic validity
4885: or implementation soundness adds claim kinds, evidence surfaces, and gate rows;
4886: upload or venue status adds external route/submission fields.  By
4887: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a genuine
4888: strengthening has a positive finite extension budget, and by
4889: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} an unpaid coordinate is
4890: reported as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the six-row exactness result cannot be
4891: read as a claim about fresh reproducibility or semantic validity; those readings
4892: require an enlarged record and a new acceptance check.\end{proof}
4893: 
4894: \begin{theorem}[Current-package extension-coordinate rigidity]
4895: \label{thm:current-package-extension-coordinate-rigidity}
4896: Assume the support-surface hypotheses of
4897: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  Let
4898: \(P^{+}_{6}\) be the accepted six-row current-package state and let
4899: \(F_{6}\) be its finite schema projection: the row identifiers
4900: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\), their tuples
4901: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the submitted support
4902: surface, the fixed dispatch table, and the recorded verifier log.  Then
4903: \(F_{6}\) is the largest current-package invariant certified by this submission
4904: before adding a new finite coordinate.  More precisely:
4905: \begin{enumerate}
4906: \item any two ambient repository, daemon, implementation, artifact, upload, or
4907: venue states with the same \(F_{6}\) have the same accepted current-package
4908: certificate set;
4909: \item no current-package reading that asserts gate-implementation soundness,
4910: fresh full-source or artifact rerun success, artifact-semantic validity,
4911: semantic theorem-inventory completeness, upload status, or venue acceptance is a
4912: consequence of \(F_{6}\);
4913: \item each such stronger reading is accepted by the publication-safety interface
4914: if and only if the submitted package adds the corresponding positive extension
4915: coordinate from Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} and the
4916: enlarged row passes the assigned gates; and
4917: \item in the present package those extension coordinates are absent, so every
4918: listed stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of the kind
4919: classified by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
4920: \end{enumerate}
4921: Consequently Theorems~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
4922: \ref{thm:finite-verifier-normal-form},
4923: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, and
4924: \ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} form a current theorem chain only
4925: for row-frozen, schema-relative, finite-record acceptance under the stated
4926: record-gate hypothesis.  The implementation-soundness statement of
4927: Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion} is not
4928: instantiated by this package; it is exactly the extension test a later package
4929: must pass after adding implementation-gate and command records.
4930: \end{theorem}
4931: 
4932: \begin{proof}
4933: The first assertion is precisely the finite-coordinate determination theorem
4934: applied to the current package.  By
4935: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the accepted certificates
4936: are the image of the six displayed rows under
4937: \(a_i\mapsto\mathcal C(a_i)\), conditioned on the submitted support surface and
4938: the recorded verifier log.  By
4939: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a verifier decision is
4940: a function only of the finite projection it reads.  Hence two ambient states with
4941: the same \(F_{6}\) produce the same current-package certificate set, regardless
4942: of unsubmitted implementation states, live daemon states, artifact worktrees,
4943: upload histories, or venue decisions.
4944: 
4945: Now let \(q\) be one of the stronger readings listed in the statement.  It is
4946: not a function of \(F_{6}\).  Gate-implementation soundness adds an
4947: implementation object, an implementation specification, source or version data,
4948: and implementation-gate or command-run evidence.  A fresh full-source or artifact
4949: rerun adds a command-run boundary tuple and usually an output surface.  Artifact
4950: semantic validity adds an artifact-semantic evidence surface and the pass row or
4951: evaluator command that checks the asserted property.  Semantic theorem-inventory
4952: completeness adds a semantic extractor or checker coordinate, source-to-output
4953: binding data, and its pass record.  Upload status and venue acceptance add
4954: external route, submission, or decision records.  None of these data is among the
4955: six row tuples and support-surface fields constituting \(F_{6}\).
4956: 
4957: Since \(q\) is not record-equivalent to the accepted finite-record reading,
4958: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} supplies a nonempty normal
4959: form
4960: \(\Delta(a_i,q)=(\Delta K,\Delta E,\Delta H,\Delta R,\Delta D,\Delta B)\)
4961: for the row or rows affected by \(q\).  If the submitted package contains that
4962: extension and the added coordinates pass the gates assigned by \(\Gamma\), the
4963: enlarged tuple satisfies the six schema clauses; by
4964: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} it is accepted exactly as an
4965: enlarged finite-record claim.  Conversely, if the interface accepts \(q\), the
4966: same theorem forces the enlarged tuple to satisfy every clause, so the relevant
4967: positive extension coordinates and pass rows must be present.  This proves the
4968: if-and-only-if criterion.
4969: 
4970: It remains only to identify the present package.  Inspecting the six-row ledger
4971: used by Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, each row is
4972: declared at the path-verified finite-record level, with command-boundary fields
4973: only for the bounded verifier logs explicitly named in the support surface.  The
4974: proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion} observes
4975: that the current package does not contain a single
4976: \(\mathsf{Impl}(c)=(K^{\mathrm{impl}},E^{\mathrm{impl}},
4977: R^{\mathrm{impl}},B^{\mathrm{impl}})\) extension record for the package gates.
4978: The current package likewise supplies no fresh artifact-semantic evaluator log,
4979: no semantic theorem-inventory completeness coordinate, and no external venue
4980: decision row.  Therefore each listed stronger reading misses at least one of the
4981: normal-form coordinates.  The finite certificate obstruction skeleton classifies
4982: the first missing coordinate as a minimal bad subrecord: missing evidence surface,
4983: missing pass row, missing route or venue boundary, missing discovery/interface
4984: row, or missing command-run tuple.  Removing that stronger reading leaves the
4985: six-row finite certificate set unchanged.\end{proof}
4986: 
4987: \begin{theorem}[Current-package semantic non-escalation]
4988: \label{thm:current-package-semantic-non-escalation}
4989: Let $P^{+}_{6}$ be the six-row current-package state of
4990: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, and assume the same
4991: support-surface availability condition.  For each accepted row $a_i$ with
4992: certificate
4993: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the following are
4994: equivalent for any proposed stronger reading $a_i^{+}$ of the same manuscript
4995: row.
4996: \begin{enumerate}
4997: \item $a_i^{+}$ is accepted by the finite-record safety interface for the current
4998: package.
4999: \item The package supplies a positive extension record
5000: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) of the normal form in
5001: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, and every nonempty
5002: coordinate of that record lies on the submitted support surface and passes its
5003: assigned gates.
5004: \end{enumerate}
5005: Consequently no row of $P^{+}_{6}$ entails, by itself, semantic correctness of
5006: BEDC source theory, Lean, Rule110/GroundCompiler, the certificate-verifier
5007: implementation, upload surfaces, or venue decisions.  In the current submission
5008: those stronger readings are certifiable only as extension claims; when the
5009: extension coordinates are absent they are rejected by a finite bad subrecord, and
5010: the accepted content remains exactly the six row-frozen finite-record certificates
5011: of Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.
5012: \end{theorem}
5013: 
5014: \begin{proof}
5015: Fix $i$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the row
5016: $a_i$ is accepted exactly through the finite tuple
5017: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\) at the declared
5018: path-verified level, and the theorem identifies no additional accepted
5019: current-package row outside the six-row image.  If $a_i^{+}$ is accepted as a
5020: properly stronger reading, then it is not record-equivalent to $a_i$ in the
5021: preorder of Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  Hence
5022: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} collects the finite
5023: differences into a positive extension record
5024: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) consisting of added claim kinds, evidence surfaces,
5025: gate-pass rows, boundary fields, discovery rows, or command-run data.  Acceptance
5026: forces each added coordinate to satisfy the corresponding schema clause A1--A6,
5027: so the extension coordinates must be submitted and gate-passing.  This proves
5028: (1)$\Rightarrow$(2).
5029: 
5030: Conversely, suppose the positive extension record is supplied on the submitted
5031: surface and every added coordinate passes its assigned gates.  Enlarging
5032: \(\mathcal C(a_i)\) by those coordinates gives the certificate tuple displayed in
5033: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The six schema clauses of
5034: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} then hold for the
5035: enlarged reading, and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts
5036: $a_i^{+}$ exactly at the declared evidence level.  This proves (2)$\Rightarrow$(1).
5037: 
5038: The listed semantic readings are all proper strengthenings of a six-row finite
5039: certificate: BEDC and Lean semantic correctness add formal-source or
5040: implementation-soundness coordinates; Rule110/GroundCompiler semantic validity
5041: adds artifact-semantic or evaluator-run coordinates; verifier implementation
5042: soundness adds implementation source, command, and pass records; upload and venue
5043: readings add external support-surface or decision fields.  None of those
5044: coordinates is one of the six row-frozen certificates identified by
5045: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  If the corresponding
5046: extension record is absent,
5047: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the first
5048: missing evidence surface, gate record, external boundary, discovery row, or
5049: command-run tuple as a finite bad subrecord.  Therefore the current-package
5050: exactness theorem is a finite-record certificate theorem and not a semantic
5051: correctness theorem for the named systems or surfaces.\end{proof}
5052: 
5053: \begin{theorem}[Named boundary positive-coordinate classification]
5054: \label{thm:named-boundary-positive-coordinate-classification}
5055: Assume the support-surface hypotheses of
5056: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  Let $F_{6}$ be the
5057: finite six-row projection of
5058: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-extension-coordinate-rigidity}.  Consider a
5059: proposed strengthened reading of one of the following six boundary interfaces:
5060: \begin{enumerate}
5061: \item reading Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} as end-to-end
5062: correctness of the AI-assisted workflow rather than conditional finite-record
5063: non-escalation;
5064: \item reading Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness}
5065: or Corollary~\ref{cor:finite-script-rgs-instantiation-boundary} as correctness of
5066: the Python interpreter, filesystem model, verifier implementations, or external
5067: artifacts rather than finite-script projection soundness;
5068: \item reading Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} or
5069: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} as a fresh
5070: full-source rebuild or semantic source-theorem import rather than the manual
5071: path-verified source-interface record;
5072: \item reading Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}
5073: or Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} as theorem semantics,
5074: proof correctness, or scope adequacy rather than syntactic theorem-environment
5075: inventory coverage;
5076: \item reading Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} or
5077: Corollary~\ref{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary} as dynamic artifact
5078: validation or artifact-substrate semantic validity rather than a manifest-bounded
5079: record theorem; or
5080: \item reading Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} or
5081: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} as mathematical-software
5082: correctness rather than byte-coordinate support closure for local support files.
5083: \end{enumerate}
5084: Then exactly one obstruction schema controls all six cases.  Such a strengthened
5085: reading is accepted by the publication-safety interface if and only if the
5086: submitted package adds a positive extension coordinate of the corresponding type:
5087: an implementation-soundness contract for (1) and (2), a deterministic
5088: full-source extractor/rebuild and semantic source-theorem check for (3), a
5089: semantic proof-audit or scope-audit checker for (4), an evaluator specification
5090: and artifact-semantic rerun log for (5), and the external upload/archive,
5091: fresh-command, or semantic-software records claimed by the stronger support
5092: reading for (6).  If the corresponding coordinate is absent, then two ambient
5093: states can agree on $F_{6}$ and disagree on the strengthened reading, and the
5094: attempted strengthening has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of one of the
5095: five types classified in
5096: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  In the current
5097: package the listed positive extension coordinates are absent, so these six
5098: interfaces contribute only their stated finite-record boundary claims to the
5099: core theorem chain.
5100: \end{theorem}
5101: 
5102: \begin{proof}
5103: First observe that the six displayed readings have the same formal shape.  Each
5104: starts with a theorem already accepted through the finite coordinates of $F_{6}$
5105: or through a support theorem cited by those coordinates, and each asks to add a
5106: fact not read by those finite coordinates.  In case (1) the added fact is global
5107: workflow correctness beyond record-gate soundness.  In case (2) it is correctness
5108: of the executable substrate or of objects outside the parsed finite projection.
5109: In case (3) it is semantic import of the pinned BEDC/newmath source rather than
5110: line-range and record comparison.  In case (4) it is semantic theorem validity or
5111: adequacy rather than syntactic environment coverage.  In case (5) it is artifact
5112: semantics rather than manifest membership.  In case (6) it is external support or
5113: software correctness rather than byte-coordinate digest closure.  None of these
5114: added facts is a component of $F_{6}$.
5115: 
5116: For the forward direction, suppose one strengthened reading is accepted.  Since
5117: it is not a function of $F_{6}$, it is a proper stronger reading in the preorder
5118: of Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The finite extension budget
5119: normal form, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, therefore
5120: assigns it a nonempty extension record consisting of added claim kinds, evidence
5121: surfaces, boundary fields, pass rows, discovery rows, or command-run data.  The
5122: schema exactness theorem,
5123: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, says that acceptance of the
5124: stronger row forces every such added coordinate to lie on the submitted support
5125: surface and to pass the gates assigned by $\Gamma$.  Inspecting the six cases
5126: identifies the required coordinate types listed in the statement: implementation
5127: and command records for global workflow or script-substrate soundness, extractor
5128: and semantic-source records for source import, semantic proof-audit records for
5129: the theorem inventory, evaluator and rerun records for artifact semantics, and
5130: external-support or fresh-command records for stronger fixed-point support
5131: claims.
5132: 
5133: For the reverse direction, suppose the corresponding positive extension
5134: coordinate is added and its assigned gates pass.  Enlarge the original finite
5135: certificate row by that coordinate.  The added evidence surface discharges clause
5136: A1 of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}; the added pass
5137: rows discharge clause A3; any human-promotion, discovery, or command-run fields
5138: introduced by the coordinate discharge clauses A4--A6; and the unchanged original
5139: row continues to discharge the remaining clauses.  Hence
5140: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts the strengthened reading at
5141: exactly the evidence level named by the added coordinate.  This proves the
5142: if-and-only-if classification.
5143: 
5144: It remains to show minimal obstruction when the coordinate is absent.  By
5145: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, verifier acceptance
5146: is a function only of the finite coordinates it reads.  Therefore one may choose
5147: two ambient states with identical $F_{6}$ but different unsubmitted facts for the
5148: relevant strengthened axis: a sound versus unsound gate implementation, a true
5149: versus false semantic theorem import, a complete versus incomplete proof audit,
5150: a valid versus invalid artifact substrate, or an uploaded versus non-uploaded
5151: support surface.  The finite verifier cannot distinguish the pair.  If it
5152: accepted the strengthened reading from $F_{6}$ alone, it would accept it in both
5153: ambient states, contradicting the assumed difference of the unsubmitted fact.
5154: Thus some finite coordinate demanded by the stronger reading is missing.  The
5155: obstruction skeleton theorem returns a minimal bad subrecord: missing evidence
5156: surface, missing assigned pass row, missing promotion or route boundary, missing
5157: discovery/interface row, or missing command-run tuple.  The present package has
5158: only the six row-frozen finite certificates of
5159: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} and the support-boundary
5160: records explicitly classified later, so the positive extension coordinates just
5161: listed are not instantiated here.\end{proof}
5162: 
5163: \begin{corollary}[Current package path-verified instantiation]
5164: \label{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}
5165: For the current presentation package, let $P$ be the finite package consisting
5166: of the primary source \path{submission_abstract.tex}, this supplement
5167: \path{main.tex}, and the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support
5168: surface of Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, together
5169: with the executable certificate records named in
5170: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.  Root-level workflow
5171: diagnostic files are local historical/path-verified context only unless their
5172: records are represented on that submitted surface.  The path catalogue
5173: in $P$, through the exact six-row certificate map of
5174: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, instantiates
5175: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, at the
5176: path-verified evidence level as follows.  The imported discovery-gate interface
5177: is fixed by the pinned newmath commit
5178: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}, the source-interface table,
5179: the JSON mirror, and the bundled source snapshot; the four case-study records
5180: are bounded witnesses for promotion, route-overlap/submission-state,
5181: theorem-content, and artifact-limitation gates; and every such case record is
5182: usable only for the bounded manuscript lesson named in its row.  The finite
5183: acceptance entries and certificate fields are exactly the rows of
5184: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}.
5185: Therefore the
5186: current package supports exactly the path-verified publication-safety claim that
5187: its load-bearing case-study assertions are reviewable, gate-typed, and equipped
5188: with locally checkable publication certificates at the path-verified level.  The
5189: corollary is historical and record-level: it instantiates the certificate
5190: constructor using the listed paths, tables, and bundle records, not by rerunning
5191: the whole workflow.  It does not instantiate any stronger claim that the full
5192: newmath source tree, a publication daemon, an artifact validator, or the Rule110
5193: dynamic artifact suite was freshly rerun.
5194: \end{corollary}
